## 5. Risk register — read this before anything else

| Risk | Severity | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Teacher–researcher conflict of interest** — you grade the people you study | 🔴 high | Consent **collected by the sociologist**, not you. Data pseudonymised and **withheld from analysis until final grades are filed**. State this in every paper's ethics note. |
| **Coercion risk** — students may feel participation affects marks | 🔴 high | Explicit opt-in/opt-out with **zero grade consequence**; opting out must be invisible to you until after grading. |
| **RGPD/GDPR** — commit histories, declarations and recordings are personal data | 🔴 high | Legal basis, retention period, pseudonymisation at ingest, UDIT data-protection officer sign-off **before** September. |
| **Institutional ethics approval** | 🟠 | Check whether UDIT has an ethics committee; if not, adopt an external protocol (e.g. ACM SIGCHI ethics guidance) and say so. |
| **Small n** | 🟠 | Do not promise inference. Frame as DBR / experience report / qualitative study. Null results are publishable in a blank. |
| **"Innovación docente" ≠ research merit** in Spanish accreditation | 🟠 | Aim at **indexed CER venues** (§6), not internal teaching-innovation calls, for the merit-bearing outputs. |
| **Novelty overclaim** | 🟡 | You are replicating known mechanisms in a new cohort. That is the honest and defensible claim. |

> **If you take one thing from this document:** consent and data governance must be in place **before the first class of 2026-27**, or the year's data is unusable. That is the single hard deadline.

